# Stable device names for the two USB devices the robot's launch files name
# directly. Install with:
#
#   sudo cp scripts/udev/99-burgerbot.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
#
# Both devices enumerate under kernel names that move: /dev/ttyACM0 and
# /dev/ttyUSB0 are assigned in probe order, so plugging the lidar in before
# the Pico silently swaps them and the robot drives on lidar data or tries to
# scan through the motor controller. Neither failure is obvious from the logs.
#
# BEFORE TRUSTING THIS FILE, CHECK YOUR OWN IDs. Vendor/product pairs differ
# between board revisions and firmware stacks, and a rule that matches nothing
# fails silently -- you get no symlink and a launch error pointing at a device
# path with no explanation of why it is absent. With the device plugged in:
#
#   udevadm info -a -n /dev/ttyACM0 | grep -m2 -E 'idVendor|idProduct'
#
# Then confirm the rules took:
#
#   ls -l /dev/burgerbot_pico /dev/rplidar
#
# GROUP="dialout" rather than MODE="0666": the ports should be readable by
# somebody in the group, not by everything on the machine. Add yourself once
# with `sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER`, then log out and back in -- group
# membership is only picked up at login, which is why this appears not to work
# until you do.

# --- Raspberry Pi Pico: motor commands and encoder counts over CDC serial ---
# Referenced as /dev/burgerbot_pico by
# burgerbot_description/urdf/burgerbot_ros2_control.xacro.
#
# 2e8a is Raspberry Pi's vendor ID. The product ID depends on what the Pico is
# running: 000a for the Pico SDK's stdio-USB, 0005 for MicroPython, 0003 while
# it is in BOOTSEL mode (which is a mass-storage device, not a tty, so it will
# not match this rule -- that is correct, there is nothing to talk to then).
# Matching on vendor alone covers all firmware choices; add
# ATTRS{idProduct}=="000a" if you have another Raspberry Pi USB device attached
# and the symlink lands on the wrong one.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="2e8a", SYMLINK+="burgerbot_pico", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660"

# --- RPLIDAR A1: scans over a CP2102 USB-UART bridge ---
# Referenced as /dev/rplidar by burgerbot_bringup/config/rplidar_a1.yaml.
#
# 10c4:ea60 is the Silicon Labs CP2102, which is what the A1's USB adapter
# uses. It is a very common bridge chip, so if you have another CP2102 device
# on the robot this will match both -- narrow it with the serial number that
# `udevadm info` reports, via ATTRS{serial}=="...".
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", SYMLINK+="rplidar", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660"
